ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor  VOTE: yellow
MANDATORY_BASELINES: 
- [{name: "LevelDB WAL Recovery", why: "LevelDB's ReadRecord truncates the WAL at the first corrupted record/EOF by default unless paranoid_checks is enabled. fjall's behavior may be exactly matching this industry-standard baseline.", repo_or_cite: "https://github.com/google/leveldb/blob/master/db/log_reader.cc"}]
- [{name: "ALICE / dm-log-writes", why: "The gold standard for proving application-level crash vulnerabilities.", repo_or_cite: "Pillai et al., OSDI '14, 'All File Systems Are Not Created Equal'"}]
STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["The 'faithful labeled reorder-SIM' manually zeroes a 4KiB page in a WAL containing subsequent SyncAll-acked batches. Under POSIX fsync semantics, if batch N+1 is fsync'd, batch N must also be persistent. An OS crash cannot leave a hole of unwritten data (page N) followed by valid fsync'd data (page N+1). Thus, this specific corruption state is unreachable via OS crashes, making the 'discarding acked data physically present after the hole' an artificial scenario unless the hardware device itself ignores flush barriers."]
MISSING_METRICS: ["Proof of reachability: demonstration that the zeroed-interior-page state can actually be produced by an OS/filesystem crash when SyncAll is used."]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Use dm-log-writes to record a real workload using SyncAll. Replay to every crash point. Demonstrate a point where SyncAll returned Ok for a key, but a crash at that specific point results in the DB silently opening without that key.
RATIONALE: The evaluation relies on a manual byte mutation to simulate a "dropped page-writeback" in a file with subsequent SyncAll-acked data. This violates the fsync barrier guarantee: an OS cannot flush page N+1 and confirm fsync without also flushing page N. The simulated state is therefore an impossible crash state under standard POSIX compliance, rendering the "silent loss of acked data" claim a strawman. The real dm-flakey test only dropped Buffer data, which carries no durability guarantee. Furthermore, truncating a WAL at the first framing error is standard LevelDB behavior. To achieve GREEN, the claim MUST use dm-log-writes to show silent loss of actually acknowledged data under a valid POSIX crash state.
